# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Telemetry export path — the ONE-DOCUMENT hybrid (`GENRE-COMPOSITION`, spec core §4).
#
# What this figure probes: a typed region NESTED INSIDE a scene document,
# with NO second `figdown` header. FigDown has two ways to put a table (or a
# bitfield, or a timing diagram) next to a box-and-wire figure, and this is
# the one that had never been demonstrated in the corpus until 0.1:
#
#   `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS`, multi-section — the file carries a second `figdown 0.1 table` header
#     and the table is a SEPARATE DOCUMENT stacked below the first.
#     See examples/evpn-fabric.fd and examples/pvlan-flows.fd.
#
#   `GENRE-COMPOSITION`, nested region (THIS FILE) — one header, one document. A `table`,
#     `bitfield` or `timing` line opens a region governed by THAT genre's
#     namespace, and the next top-level directive of the host genre closes
#     it. No `end` keyword: scope is sticky, exactly as inside a genre's own
#     document.
#
# The difference is not cosmetic. Under `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS` the reader gets two documents with
# two `sections` entries and no relation between them. Under `GENRE-COMPOSITION` it gets ONE
# document whose region list stands in document order — still no declared
# relation (that is `CROSS-BLOCK-REFERENCES`, v0.2), but one figure, one title, one artifact.
# Reach for `GENRE-COMPOSITION` when the panel is PART OF the figure, and for `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS` when it is a
# second figure that merely travels with the first.
#
# Composition is NOT inheritance (core §4). This document is `block`, so
# `node`/`edge`/`class` are its vocabulary; `|` rows, `cell` and `width` are
# valid ONLY inside the `table` region below, and `field` would be a line
# error anywhere in this file because no `bitfield` region is open.
figdown 0.1 block
title "Telemetry export path"

class lossy "Lossy by design — this stage drops under back-pressure rather than blocking the datapath" stroke=#b91c1c style=dashed

# ── Scene region: the export pipeline itself ─────────────────────────────
external asic "from the forwarding ASIC"
node sample "Sampler" shape=rounded
node ring "Export ring" shape=cylinder class=lossy
node enc "gRPC encoder" shape=box
external coll "to the collector"

edge asic -[1 in 2048]-> sample
edge sample -> ring
edge ring -[drop on full]-> enc class=lossy
edge enc -> coll

# `flow up`. The export path climbs OUT of the datapath: the ASIC is at the
# bottom of the figure because it is at the bottom of the stack, and each
# stage above it is further from the wire. Twelve figures in this corpus flow
# `right` and twelve flow `down`; `up` and `left` had no demonstrator at all
# before 0.1, so half of a four-value enum went into the freeze
# untested by any figure. (`left` is in examples/rpf-check.fd.)
flow up
rank ring,enc

# ── Nested table region — opens here, still the SAME document ────────────
# The `table` keyword is in `block`'s top-level namespace precisely so a scene
# can compose one. Everything from here to the next `block` directive reads
# under the `table` genre: the `|` rows, `cell` and `width`.
table budget "Per-stage export budget"
| Stage        | Rate (pps) | Drops/min |
|--------------|-----------:|----------:|
| Sampler      |      12000 |         0 |
| Export ring  |       9600 |       410 |
| gRPC encoder |       9600 |         0 |
# `cell` addresses the region's own grid — the region is a complete figure of
# its genre, not a degraded fragment of one.
cell (2,3) fill=#fee2e2
width auto,90,90

# ── Nested bitfield region — the SECOND composable genre, same document ──
# `table` is not a special case: `bitfield` and `timing` open a region in a
# scene document on exactly the same rule, and each is governed by its own
# genre inside it. `field` is a line error anywhere above this line and legal
# from here to the next `block` directive.
#
# The region's caption is written EMPTY (`EMPTY-LABEL-STATE`). The host figure has already
# named this thing — it is the record `enc` writes into the export ring — so a
# caption would repeat it, and the third label state exists precisely so the
# author can record "none, deliberately" instead of omitting the key and
# leaving a reader to wonder whether the caption was lost.
bitfield rec "" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "Version" 4
field "Type" 4
field "Length" 24
field "Observation Domain" 32
field "Records" *
# `chart` is EXPERIMENTAL (core §4.4): it is outside the v0.1 compatibility
# promise and may change or leave. It reads an existing table by id and draws
# it, so it needs no data of its own — and it is listed in the `block` and
# `topology` vocabularies exactly so a scene document can carry one over a
# table it composed. That row had no demonstrator anywhere in the corpus
# before 0.1, which is the whole reason this line is here. Every column
# after the first must be numeric — a chart plots the numbers, and the label
# column names the series, so the `Owner` column this table would otherwise
# carry belongs in a `description` or a second table, not here.
chart budget type=bar3d
